y  different things from seas to pluck homo  son cumali nil and they Ally ennum Toto  for this saying from the early Roman  comedian Terence is genius for plain  eloquence the English version is 50%  longer in terms of words and nothing  like as memorable especially when it  comes to word order the ability to  manipulate word order is a great  advantage that Latin gains from its  grammatical inflections the way nouns  and adjectives incorporate their own  cases and verbs include person mood and  tense within a single word here being  able to hold back the verb to the end of  the sentence enables parents to  juxtapose the home assume with the  Romani nil parturient Montez Ness  scatter ridiculous muss here from  Horace's ARS Poetica his hugely  influential treatise on how to write is  a lovely example of Latin being playful  as it were using its own weightiness  against itself Latin is able to enact  the process whereby a huge effort the  mountains going into labor produces a  laughably puny result the birth of the  minuscule unimpressive rodent cap acts  in pari  Nissi impure asset the master of the  withering liters put down in Latin or  maybe in all languages is the Roman  historian Tacitus this marvelous  forwarder refers to the emperor Galba  but could be seen as an equally fitting  epitaph on at least one recent British  prime minister here both succeeds  enabled by grammar and the unique Latin  weightiness and a perfect expression  caelum non-animal mutant qui trans Mari  current  another Parisian Jen this time from his  first book of epistles depends once  again for its memorable force on the  genius of Latin grammar the way the  accusative tense is signals in the final  owners of caelum and animam so that the  contrast between the climate or color of  the sky caelum which the traveler can  change and her state of mind animam  which the traveler can't alter so easily  is pointed up with a maximum impact  latin more than any other tongue is the  language in which things are not just  said but stay said the tight grammatical  structure of the language helps give  Latin sayings a massive architectural  grandeur the verbal equivalent of a  Colosseum the pantheon or the pond ooga  like those great edifices they are built  to stand the test of time we publish new  thought-provoking films every week be  sure to subscribe to our Channel and  take a look at more of what we have to  offer at the link on your screen now
 

